  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1222 PM CDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nThis Afternoon - Tonight: Breezy and Predominantly Dry\n\nAs this morning's showers and storms continue to move eastward,\npredominantly dry conditions will take hold as we head into \ntonight. Along with the dry conditions, an increased low level \nwind field (35-40kts at 925hPa) associated with a strengthening \nlow level jet will allow for breezy conditions through the early\nafternoon hours, mainly west of the Mississippi River and south\nof I-90. Gusts should top out around 30-35mph, though some \ngusts of 40mph are possible should mixing be slightly deeper \nthan currently expected with probabilities of 40mph gusts \nsitting around 20-40% per the 29.12z HREF.\n\nDespite the predominantly dry conditions, there is low \npotential for scattered showers this evening and into tonight as\nWAA in the 925-850hPa layer and 850hPa moisture transport \nincrease associated with the previously mentioned low level jet.\nProbabilities for these showers are highest north of I-94 \n(20-40%) where deeper saturation is present within 29.15z \nHRRR/RAP soundings. Further south, drier air below 10kft should \nlargely inhibit rainfall from reaching the surface though the \nforcing may be able to overcome this lack of saturation,\nespecially into central Wisconsin as the low level jet begins to\npivot to the east. Can't rule out some rumbles of thunder \neither as these same model soundings do highlight some elevated \ninstability (MUCAPE of 500-750 J/kg) across the region.\n\nSunday - Sunday Night: Shower and Storm Potential\n\nThe main story for Sunday is the potential for another round of\nshowers and storms in the afternoon. 850hPa moisture transport \nand WAA noses into a warm front draped across the region, \ngenerally along I-90, which is expected to provide enough \nforcing for convective development along and south of this \nboundary. Instability looks to be on the lower side given the \nexpected elevated nature of the showers/storms with MLCAPE \nsitting around 500-1000 J/kg across our region, higher to the\nnorthwest across central Minnesota. The more favorable \nkinematic environment for strong storms is also expected to \nexist north and west of the area, which is in line with where \nthe SPC and AI/ML guidance highlights the greatest potential for\na stronger storm.\n\nEarly Next Week: Warmer, Periodic Shower and Storm Chances Continue\n\nTemperatures warm as we head into the beginning of the new week\nas persistent southerly low level winds continue to usher \nwarmer air into the Upper Midwest and mid to upper level ridging\nremains across the central United States. An 850hPa thermal \nridge axis sets-up across the region Monday with temperatures at\nthis pressure level reaching 22-24C, translating to surface \nhighs in the upper 80s with little spread in the 25th-75th \npercentiles of the GEFS/EPS/GEPS and their AI counterparts. The \nwarmth will continue during the overnight hours when some near \nrecord warm low could be achieved, mainly across northeast Iowa.\n\nDespite the upper ridge remaining in place, a shortwave trough \nwill begin to flatten it on Monday, allowing additional \nshortwaves to traverse the mean flow through the middle of next \nweek. Periods of showers and storms will develop in response to \nthese upper level features with the potential for some stronger \nstorms, particularly on Monday when AI/ML guidance has broad low\nend probabilities for strong storm potential, though details \nare murky as far as when/where storms occur.",
